# 15. MULTI-PROVIDER AI ARCHITECTURE AND IRAN COMPATIBILITY

This is one of the most important technical requirements.

AI services may be filtered, restricted, unavailable, or blocked in Iran.

The application must use a flexible AI provider architecture.

Create a common AI provider interface.

For example:

- AIProvider
- GeminiProvider
- AlternativeAIProvider
- AIProviderManager

The provider manager must support configurable provider priorities.

Example priority:

1. Primary AI provider.
2. Secondary AI provider.
3. Third available provider.

When a provider is unavailable because of:

- Network failure.
- Timeout.
- Service outage.
- Rate limit.
- Regional restriction.
- Authentication failure.

The manager should attempt the next eligible provider when appropriate.

Use reasonable connection and read timeouts.

Avoid endless retry loops.

Use exponential backoff with limited retry attempts.

Do not repeatedly retry requests when the device has no internet connection.

Do not block the UI while trying different providers.

Display the actual connection status to the user.

## Critical security requirements

NEVER:

- Hardcode API keys in Kotlin source files.
- Embed secret API keys in the APK.
- Expose provider credentials in logs.
- Store API secrets in DataStore or SharedPreferences.
- Disable TLS certificate validation.
- Accept arbitrary certificates.
- Use insecure HTTP connections.
- Disable authentication or App Check just to make an AI request work.

Prefer official, supported secure integrations.

For Gemini, use an appropriate secure integration such as Firebase AI Logic with properly configured authentication and App Check where applicable.

For other providers, only enable integrations that can be implemented without exposing secrets.

Since this application must not require a personal backend server, do not introduce a custom backend merely to make the integration appear functional.

If a particular provider requires a secure backend to protect its API credentials, do not embed its secret in the application as a workaround.

Keep that provider disabled until a secure integration is configured.

Never claim that a provider is available until a real connection has been verified.

## Fix the current AI security error

The application currently displays:

"AI service security settings could not be verified. If you are using a proxy server, check your server configuration."

Investigate and fix the actual cause.

Inspect:

- Firebase project configuration, if Firebase AI Logic is used.
- Correct project and application configuration.
- Package name and signing configuration.
- Required API enablement.
- Firebase AI Logic initialization.
- Authentication configuration.
- App Check configuration.
- Provider endpoint configuration.
- Network security configuration.
- TLS configuration.
- Request authorization.
- Any proxy or gateway configuration currently present.

Do not simply hide the error message.

Do not bypass security verification.

Do not weaken application security rules.

If the error originates from an incorrectly configured service, correct the actual configuration and clearly identify any external setup that still requires the developer's action.

## Internet connectivity handling

Before an AI request:

1. Check network connectivity.
2. Verify that internet access is actually available.
3. Attempt the selected provider.
4. If unavailable, try the next securely configured provider.
5. If all providers fail, show a clear message.

If the internet is disconnected, display:

"اتصال اینترنت برقرار نیست. لطفاً اینترنت خود را روشن کنید."

If no AI provider can be reached, display:

"در حال حاضر اتصال به سرویس هوش مصنوعی امکان‌پذیر نیست. لطفاً بعداً دوباره تلاش کنید."

In English, provide equivalent messages.

Do not crash the application.

Do not freeze the interface.

Do not lose user-entered information.

All non-AI functionality must continue working normally.

